1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.303
In previous lessons, you constructed a knowledge graph

2
00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.872
that integrates APIs with additional information

3
00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:09.476
about business processes that involve those APIs.

4
00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:11.878
In this lesson, you will learn

5
00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.880
how knowledge graphs can be used

6
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.983
for API discovery for AI agents.

7
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:20.387
Let's dive in. Here's what you'll do in this lesson.

8
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:23.990
During design time, you will do initial preparation for retrieval,

9
00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:27.494
and at run time, you receive and process user queries.

10
00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:30.597
So at design time, you retrieve entity sets,

11
00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:33.366
their labels and properties from the

12
00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:36.102
knowledge graph. You will then use embedding

13
00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:39.739
model to turn retrieved texts into embedding vectors.

14
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:42.909
And at run time, you'll start with a flat index retrieval

15
00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:44.911
based on embedding texts.

16
00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:48.915
You will then add APIs connected by business process edges.

17
00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.918
Finally, we enrich the retrieval

18
00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:54.921
based on the business process information from the Knowledge Graph.

19
00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:58.358
With this background, let's dive into this notebook.

20
00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:03.063
As in the previous lessons, let's import all the necessary packages first.

21
00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.766
We also have some new packages as part of this notebook.

22
00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:09.369
For example, we import faiss index classes

23
00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.739
for performing semantic similarity search on entity sets.

24
00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.275
We also import tqdm,

25
00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:17.777
a utility to show progress bars for loops.

26
00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:21.948
We then import OpenAI powered text embeddings via LangChain.

27
00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:24.150
And just as in previous lessons,

28
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.953
we have a helper function for parameterizing SPARQL queries.

29
00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:28.755
All right, let's now set up

30
00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:30.256
our data frames to be displayed nicely.

31
00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.860
Let's begin by setting up a LangChain embedding model

32
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.063
using OpenAI's text-embedding-3-large model.

33
00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:39.265
Next, we load the knowledge graph

34
00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.067
we constructed in the previous lessons.

35
00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:46.106
In real world business scenarios, we often work with thousands of APIs.

36
00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:49.476
Presenting all of these options to a large language model

37
00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:53.680
is typically neither efficient due to token limitations and latency,

38
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.982
nor is it practical in most cases.

39
00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:59.285
Therefore, we need to reduce the API selection space

40
00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.788
to a small and relevant subset

41
00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:04.090
for the agent for a given user

42
00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.192
query. Doing the flat retrieval

43
00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:08.895
reduces the number of APIs to some extent,

44
00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:10.597
but poses some problems.

45
00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:12.599
Number one, false negatives.

46
00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:15.902
These are relevant APIs that are not semantically

47
00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:18.104
close enough to the user query.

48
00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:20.607
So the retrieval step misses them.

49
00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:23.610
By enhancing our retrieval with a second step

50
00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.513
using business process edges from the knowledge graph.

51
00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:29.015
those APIs can still be reached.

52
00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:33.019
We complete the business processes that are touched by the true positives

53
00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:36.322
with the help of the process information from the knowledge graph.

54
00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.691
Number two is false positives.

55
00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.295
Adding the business process information on top of just

56
00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:45.298
the flat list of retrieved API endpoints

57
00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:48.301
helps the agent to disregard the false positives.

58
00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:52.505
Before you construct embedding text for entity sets,

59
00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.508
let's first recall that for each entity set,

60
00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:58.511
you store all its properties in the knowledge graph.

61
00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:02.182
The first step is to prepare a SPARQL query

62
00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:04.184
to retrieve all the properties

63
00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:06.186
for our example entity set

64
00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:07.887
related to purchase order.

65
00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:10.256
So let's briefly review what we created here.

66
00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.992
The variable q_api_properties

67
00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.795
stores the prepared SPARQL query.

68
00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:19.599
These define the short prefixes for rdf,

69
00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:22.001
xsd, and odata namespaces.

70
00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:27.268
Here you select distinct pairs of entity_set and property_label,

71
00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:31.377
and in the WHERE clause, we'll bind the entity_set_uri

72
00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:33.680
to the purchase order entity set.

73
00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.784
Further, the triple ensures that the bound URI is explicitly

74
00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:40.253
declared as an entity set.

75
00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:43.456
And this retrieves the name of the entity set,

76
00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:45.458
which is in our case the purchase order.

77
00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:48.027
In the end, for every entity type,

78
00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:51.331
you get all the properties and their labels.

79
00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:55.735
Let's now execute this
prepared Sparql query
on our knowledge graph.

80
00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:59.105
The results of this
query are stored in a dictionary

81
00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:02.575
with two keys,
entity set and property label.

82
00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:07.247
Now let's display the first few rows
of this dictionary to see how this looks.

83
00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.717
As you can see, for the entity
set purchase order,

84
00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:13.820
we retrieved a few properties and their labels.

85
00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:16.990
For example, Address Number, Incoterms,

86
00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:19.959
purchasing organization,
quotation date, and so on.

87
00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:23.396
Next, we prepare a SPARQL query to retrieve

88
00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:25.431
and concatenate the name of an

89
00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.800
entity set and names of its properties.

90
00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:30.136
The result of this query will

91
00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:32.405
then be used to construct text embeddings.

92
00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:34.941
Here the variable q_embedding_string

93
00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:37.944
stores the prepared SPARQL query.

94
00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.213
Here we fetch and concatenate

95
00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:43.516
each entity set with the names of its properties.

96
00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.518
Let's take an example.

97
00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:48.121
to see how such an embedding text looks like.

98
00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:51.524
Here for example, you'll see for the entity set BillingDocument,

99
00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:53.926
you get several properties

100
00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.628
such as document number,

101
00:04:55.995 --> 00:05:00.099
Posting Status, Value Days, Billing Date, and so on.

102
00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.001
Now, this is just a glimpse

103
00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:04.103
of the flexibility that Knowledge Graphs offer.

104
00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:07.674
From each entity set, you could go further into the graph

105
00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:11.778
and include, for example, more information about each property

106
00:05:11.878 --> 00:05:14.881
like its data type, max length, and so on.

107
00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:17.884
Or you could include additional business process information

108
00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:20.186
that is also stored in the graph.

109
00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.290
Let's now generate text embeddings for each of the entity sets.

110
00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:29.662
Here we loop over the text that our q_embedding_string returned

111
00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:33.866
and run them through the text embedding model that we initialized earlier.

112
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.337
Okay. Now that we have the embeddings for the entity sets,

113
00:05:37.637 --> 00:05:40.440
let's index them for similarity search.

114
00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.243
We're using faiss to index the embeddings here for simplicity.

115
00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:46.646
But really any vector database works.

116
00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:49.649
Let's now save the faiss index

117
00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:52.352
and the corresponding entity_set_uris

118
00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.654
to disk with pickle.

119
00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:57.357
Let's take a look at what you did so far.

120
00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:00.026
We first query the knowledge graph

121
00:06:00.226 --> 00:06:03.229
to retrieve a subgraph per entity set,

122
00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:07.533
which includes its label and the labels of its properties.

123
00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:10.636
The retrieved labels are concatenated

124
00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.839
and the resulting string is embedded.

125
00:06:12.839 --> 00:06:14.841
The embeddings are indexed

126
00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:18.344
and associated with the corresponding entity set URI.

127
00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:20.213
Now that you've created the index, you

128
00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:22.281
can use it to find the nearest neighbors

129
00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:24.484
of a given query embedding.

130
00:06:24.584 --> 00:06:27.186
So let's define a function to do just that.

131
00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:30.189
This function takes a natural language query,

132
00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:34.193
converts it into a vector using the embedding model

133
00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:36.896
and searches the faiss index for the top k

134
00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:40.299
most matching similar entity sets and returns them.

135
00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.102
Let's try this with an example query.

136
00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.406
Here, the query we have at hand

137
00:06:46.606 --> 00:06:48.408
is the one we started with.

138
00:06:48.708 --> 00:06:50.810
To create a purchase order for five pencils

139
00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.379
in purchasing group 002

140
00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:56.382
and purchasing organization 3000.

141
00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.751
Let's execute that.

142
00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.987
You see that a lot of purchase order related

143
00:07:00.987 --> 00:07:03.456
entity sets are among the top five matching entity sets,

144
00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.558
which is what we would expect.

145
00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:07.427
We could add an additional reranking step

146
00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:09.262
here, but for now we skip that.

147
00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:12.131
An important thing to note here is that

148
00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:14.467
the retrieval step missed the purchase requisition header,

149
00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:17.069
although you see purchase requisition item here.

150
00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:20.406
And purchase requisition header is an important prerequisite

151
00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:22.608
to create a purchase order.

152
00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:26.078
So the agent would not know about it if we stopped here.

153
00:07:26.379 --> 00:07:30.116
So let's continue. Let's review what you did so far.

154
00:07:30.817 --> 00:07:34.821
You retrieved entity sets, their labels and properties from the knowledge graph.

155
00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:37.690
You then used embedding model to

156
00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.226
turn the retrieved texts into embedding vectors.

157
00:07:40.927 --> 00:07:44.831
You created a flat index retrieval based on embedding texts

158
00:07:44.831 --> 00:07:46.833
and performed sample queries.

159
00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:51.804
Now, you will add APIs connected by Business Process edges.

160
00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:54.507
And finally, you will enrich the retrieval

161
00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:58.511
based on process information from the knowledge graph.

162
00:07:58.878 --> 00:08:00.980
Okay, let's see this in action.

163
00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.182
We want to get all the process edges

164
00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:06.686
that are connected to the entity sets we retrieved earlier.

165
00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:10.289
Here, the variable stores the prepared SPARQL query.

166
00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:13.659
The select statement here retrieves

167
00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.262
unique pairs of entity set A,

168
00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:19.565
which is the source entity set, entity set B,

169
00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:21.968
the target entity set and their names.

170
00:08:22.368 --> 00:08:25.204
This query looks for process dependencies

171
00:08:25.204 --> 00:08:28.040
using the pr:hasNext property

172
00:08:28.241 --> 00:08:30.376
and then performs a union

173
00:08:30.376 --> 00:08:33.212
to combine the results from both the directions.

174
00:08:33.312 --> 00:08:35.882
Now let's look at what this results in

175
00:08:35.882 --> 00:08:37.884
after executing this against our knowledge graph.

176
00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:41.821
Let's now define a function to execute SPARQL query

177
00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:43.823
on our knowledge graph.

178
00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:46.826
This function get_process_dependencies

179
00:08:47.026 --> 00:08:49.028
takes a list of entity sets

180
00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:53.533
specified by their URIs, executes this SPARQL query

181
00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:56.369
on the graph that we loaded earlier

182
00:08:56.369 --> 00:09:00.273
to retrieve all the process dependencies from the graph

183
00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:03.643
that either lead to or originate from one of the entity sets.

184
00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:06.846
Now let's test this on the entity sets

185
00:09:06.846 --> 00:09:09.148
that we retrieved for our example query.

186
00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:13.419
a query being creating a purchase order for five pencils

187
00:09:13.653 --> 00:09:17.056
in purchasing group 002 and purchasing organization 3000.

188
00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:20.359
As you can see, we get a list of URIs

189
00:09:20.359 --> 00:09:23.563
and names of entity sets for purchase requisition

190
00:09:23.563 --> 00:09:26.065
and purchase order. And you can read

191
00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:28.568
recollect that the purchase requisition header entity

192
00:09:28.568 --> 00:09:30.770
set was missing in the previous step,

193
00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:34.273
but now with this step, we have the purchase requisition

194
00:09:34.273 --> 00:09:36.876
header also added to the list,

195
00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:40.179
This is due to the process information added in the graph.

196
00:09:40.613 --> 00:09:43.382
Okay, now that we have all the necessary building blocks,

197
00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:47.019
let's put them together and finalize our knowledge graph enhanced

198
00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:50.022
API discovery process.

199
00:09:50.323 --> 00:09:52.325
In this function, we combine

200
00:09:52.325 --> 00:09:56.829
the embedding based retrieval along with the business process dependencies.

201
00:09:57.296 --> 00:09:59.699
This takes a user input query

202
00:10:00.299 --> 00:10:03.302
and returns the most relevant entity sets

203
00:10:03.703 --> 00:10:06.305
along with their business process information.

204
00:10:06.906 --> 00:10:10.409
Let's try it out with our same sample query we used earlier.

205
00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.012
And with that, we get a list

206
00:10:13.012 --> 00:10:15.414
of matching entity sets related to purchase order,

207
00:10:15.514 --> 00:10:20.119
and most importantly, the entity set related to purchase requisition header,

208
00:10:20.219 --> 00:10:23.723
which was missing when we ran only an embedding-based retrieval.

209
00:10:24.056 --> 00:10:27.259
This, along with the business process information

210
00:10:27.259 --> 00:10:30.563
that PurchaseOrder depends on purchase requisition.

211
00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.833
Now let's recap what you learned in this lesson.

212
00:10:34.033 --> 00:10:36.235
At design time, you ran SPARQL queries

213
00:10:36.235 --> 00:10:38.838
on the knowledge graph to turn properties

214
00:10:38.838 --> 00:10:41.941
of entity sets into textual representations.

215
00:10:42.441 --> 00:10:44.543
From these textual representations,

216
00:10:44.844 --> 00:10:47.847
you computed embeddings with an embedding model.

217
00:10:48.114 --> 00:10:51.717
At runtime, the discovery starts with a flat index retrieval,

218
00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:54.320
which gives us a list of entity sets,

219
00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:58.224
but may miss some relevant ones. Then, you

220
00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:00.593
add those APIs that are connected

221
00:11:00.593 --> 00:11:03.663
to our retrieved APIs via business processes.

222
00:11:04.363 --> 00:11:06.332
In our example, we were able

223
00:11:06.332 --> 00:11:08.567
to add purchase requisition in this way.

224
00:11:09.068 --> 00:11:12.271
And finally, you add a business process information

225
00:11:12.271 --> 00:11:14.273
to the discovered entity sets.

226
00:11:14.774 --> 00:11:18.277
In the next lesson, you will apply what you just built

227
00:11:18.277 --> 00:11:21.981
to discover and execute APIs with AI agents.
